In Java, if two threads call `Singleton.getInstance()` at the same time, it could result in two instances of the Singleton class being created. To prevent this, we can use the double-checked locking mechanism or the Bill Pugh Singleton pattern.

**Java Thread-Safe Singleton Pattern Example (Double-Checked Locking)**
